@startuml
!include style.puml
title Boot sequence

participant "Home Assistant" as ha $ha
participant "Broker" as b $broker
participant "Worker k" as w $worker
participant "App" as app $usercode

== connect and seed ==
b -> ha : WebSocket connect
ha --> b : auth_required
b -> ha : auth { access_token }
ha --> b : auth_ok { ha_version }
b -> ha : subscribe_events
note right of b : changes buffered until the seed
b -> ha : get_config
ha --> b : time_zone
b -> ha : get_states
ha --> b : all entity states
b -> b : seed StateCache,\nreplay buffered changes

== spawn ==
b -> w ** : ProcessContextFactory::start(worker.php)
note right of b
  Explicitly ProcessContextFactory.
  DefaultContextFactory silently uses
  **threads** when ext-parallel exists.
  The handle must be held for the
  worker's whole life — its destructor
  kills the process.
end note
b -> w : drain stdout/stderr
b -> w : **Bootstrap** { workerId, apps[], timeZone }
w -> app ** : constructApps()  <<from the app container>>
note right of w
  Constructed but **not** initialized:
  the state cache is not seeded yet.
end note

b -> w : **StateSnapshot** { pre-encoded json }
w -> w : load state cache **in place**\n(contexts already hold the object)

== initialize ==
w -> app : initialize()
app -> w : watchEvents('call_service')->subscribe(...)
w -> b : **Subscribe** { selector, kind }
b -> b : register + invalidate match cache
b -> w : **SubscriptionAck**
app -> w : getState()  <<local hash lookup>>
w --> b : **WorkerReady** { appIds, failedAppIds, memoryBytes }
note over b
  The broker does not wait for ready: a worker
  gets every state change from spawn on.
  WorkerReady cancels its ready deadline.
end note
@enduml
